    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 1,
      "content": "Inline Vs. Block Elements"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Consider this code:"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "html",
      "content": "1<h1>Heading</h1>\n2<p>A paragraph</p>"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "If you run the code, you can see these two tags are displayed on separate lines by default."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Now, let's run this code:"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "html",
      "content": "1<strong>Strong</strong>\n2<strong>Even stronger</strong>"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Here, both tags are displayed on the same line."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "The reason is that tags such as <h1>, <p> and <ul> are block-level elements by default. Meaning they take the whole block."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "In contrast, <strong> is an inline element that doesn't extend the whole block."
    },
    {
      "type": "image",
      "alt": "Idea Emoji",
      "src": "data:image/png;base64,"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "content": "How does it work?"
